  "verbalization": "Bleomycin is linked to SH-4 through a series of molecular interactions, with the core mechanism involving the targeting of a shared intermediate, (4R)-4-(6-methylhepta-1,5-dien-2-yl)-1-(4-methylpent-3-enyl)cyclohexene. This compound is identified as a target of both bleomycin and HCE-T, suggesting that the drug may act through a common pathway to modulate the activity of this intermediate. The presence of SH-4 as a target of this intermediate implies that bleomycin may influence the function of SH-4 through direct or indirect interactions, potentially altering cellular processes that depend on this compound. The evidence suggests that the relationship between bleomycin and SH-4 is mediated by a shared target, with the drug acting as a modulator of the downstream effects of this intermediate. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of this intermediate in the biological response to bleomycin, underscoring its role as a critical node in the drug's mechanism of action.",
